// multifile_probe: a local source package split across TWO files.
//
// Goo could not compile any package of more than one file — not an imported
// package, and not `package main` itself. The pieces were half in place:
// resolve_package_dir (import_resolver.c) already globbed the directory,
// excluded *_test.go, and qsort'ed the result into a deterministic N-file
// list. But concat_package_sources (goo.c) then joined those N files into one
// text buffer, and parser.y's `program` rule accepts exactly ONE
// package_clause, so the concatenation died on the second file's clause:
//
//   Parse error at concat.goo:9:8: syntax error
//
// The path had never been exercised because all 11 goostd packages are
// single-file — strings.go is 555 lines and lanes.go is 1032, both already
// straining against a limit nobody could hit.
//
// Fixed by parsing each file separately and keeping N ASTs per package
// instead of concatenating. Keeping the real filename per file is
// load-bearing, not cosmetic: PRs #221-#223 made diagnostics report true
// line:column, and a concatenated buffer would have silently reported
// concat-relative lines the moment multi-file shipped.
//
// Expected output produced by `go run` on the equivalent Go program
// (go1.26.1), per the project convention that accept goldens are never
// hand-written.
package main

import "fmt"
import "./multifile"

func main() {
	// Shout is declared in a.go and calls Bang(), declared in b.go — the
	// cross-file reference inside one package.
	fmt.Println(multifile.Shout("hi"))

	// Twice is declared in b.go, the SECOND file, and is exported across the
	// package boundary from there.
	fmt.Println(multifile.Twice(21))
}
